\title{The Partial Free-Witness Threshold for Semiprimes:\\
A Sharp Divisibility Criterion}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For an integer $N$ and an exponent $k \ge 1$, the \emph{free witness of order
$k$} is the divisor power sum $\sigma_k(N) = \sum_{d \mid N} d^{\,k}$, which
for a semiprime $N = pq$ with distinct primes $p,q$ equals $(1+p^k)(1+q^k)$.
It is classical that the full value of $\sigma_2(N)$ determines the
factorisation of $N$: the trace $t = p+q$ satisfies $t^2 = \sigma_2(N) + 2N -
N^2 - 1$, after which $p$ and $q$ are the roots of $X^2 - tX + N$. We study
the \emph{partial} problem: given only the residue $\sigma_k(N) \bmod m$, for
which moduli $m$ is the factorisation still uniquely determined?

Our main result is a sharp threshold theorem with no size condition
whatsoever: for a semiprime $N = pq$, the residue $\sigma_k(N) \bmod m$
determines the factorisation if and only if $m$ does not divide the
\emph{witness gap} $G_k(p,q) = (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$. This is an instance of a
general \emph{separation principle}: two factorisations $N = ab = cd$ have
congruent witnesses modulo $m$ exactly when $m$ divides the difference
$(a^k+b^k)-(c^k+d^k)$ of their power-sum (trace) coordinates.

The threshold theorem refutes a conjectured law of the form $m^\star =
\Theta(p+q)$, numerically $m^\star = 5(p+q)$, observed on semiprimes of bit
length $14$--$26$. We prove instead that the fixed modulus $m=7$ suffices for
infinitely many semiprimes with both factors arbitrarily large, via
Dirichlet's theorem applied to the progression $2 \bmod 7$. We complement this
with the matching universal lower bound $m^\star \ge 5$ for $p,q>3$ (a
consequence of $24 \mid p^2-1$) and the exact characterisation $m^\star = 5$
if and only if $p,q \not\equiv \pm1 \pmod 5$. At order $k=1$ the gap is
Euler's totient, $G_1 = \varphi(N)$, so the obstruction to
partial-information factoring is precisely the RSA trapdoor. Counting bounds
show that only $O(\log N)$ prime moduli ever need to be tried, since
$\omega(G_k) \le \log_2 G_k$. All statements are information-theoretic:
computing $\sigma_k(N) \bmod m$ still requires the full divisor-sum
aggregation, so the collapse of the informational requirement to a constant
does not yield a factoring shortcut---but it sharpens the target of any
aggregation-hardness proof.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Setting}

Let $N = pq$ be a semiprime, the product of two distinct primes. It is
classical that certain \emph{global} arithmetic functionals of $N$ are
equivalent to the factorisation: knowing $\varphi(N)$, or $\sigma_1(N)$, or
the Carmichael function $\lambda(N)$, suffices to recover $p$ and $q$ in
polynomial time. Each of these is ``free'' in the sense of being canonically
attached to $N$---no auxiliary hint or oracle choice is involved---and each is
nonetheless believed to be as hard to compute as factoring itself.

We call $\sigma_k(N) = \sum_{d\mid N} d^{\,k}$ the \emph{free witness of order
$k$}. This paper quantifies exactly how much of a free witness is needed to
determine a factorisation: fix a modulus $m$ and reveal only $\sigma_k(N)
\bmod m$; when does this residue admit a unique consistent factorisation
of $N$?

\subsection{The conjecture under test}

The trace $t = p+q$ has size $\Theta(\sqrt N)$, whereas $\sigma_2(N) =
\Theta(N^2)$. Since the recovery algorithm reads $t$ off from $\sigma_2(N)$, a
natural guess is that the modulus must be large enough to represent a trace,
i.e.\ $m^\star = \Theta(p+q)$. Numerical experiments over semiprimes of bit
length $14$ to $26$ reported an apparently exact constant $m^\star/(p+q) =
5.00$, supported by the heuristic that for candidate traces $t' = (p+q)+jm$
the discriminant
\[
  t'^2 - 4N = (p-q)^2 + 2jm(p+q) + j^2m^2
\]
is generically a non-square, so that the true trace is isolated once $m$ is
large enough. We show that this heuristic, though locally accurate as a
description of a lifting search, does not describe the truth. The correct
criterion is a divisibility condition with no metric content, under which
$m^\star$ is bounded by an absolute constant infinitely often.

\subsection{Summary of results}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Witness formula and rigidity.} $\sigma_k(pq) = (1+p^k)(1+q^k)$;
  and for any $k \ge 1$ the pair (product, order-$k$ witness) is a complete
  invariant of an unordered pair of non-negative integers.
\item \textbf{Exact recovery.} For $N = pq$ with $p<q$ prime, $t =
  \sqrt{\sigma_2(N)+2N-N^2-1} = p+q$, $d = \sqrt{t^2-4N} = q-p$, and $p =
  (t-d)/2$, $q = (t+d)/2$; equivalently $X^2-tX+N = (X-p)(X-q)$.
\item \textbf{Separation principle.} For $ab = cd$, the order-$k$ witnesses
  are congruent mod $m$ iff $m \mid (a^k+b^k)-(c^k+d^k)$.
\item \textbf{Sharp threshold.} $\sigma_k(N) \bmod m$ determines the
  factorisation of the semiprime $N=pq$ iff $m \nmid (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$.
\item \textbf{Refutation of the trace law.} For every $B$ there are primes $B
  < p < q$ with $m=7$ determining; already $N = 187 = 11\cdot 17$ is
  determined mod $7 \ll p+q = 28$.
\item \textbf{Lower bound and exact-five locus.} For $p,q>3$ no $m \le 4$
  determines; and $m^\star = 5$ iff $p,q \not\equiv \pm1 \pmod 5$.
\item \textbf{Totient at order one.} $\sigma_1(N) \bmod m$ determines iff $m
  \nmid \varphi(N)$.
\item \textbf{Counting bounds.} $\omega(G_k) \le \log_2 G_k$; any family of
  more than $\omega(G_k)$ distinct primes contains a determining modulus.
\end{itemize}

\section{Definitions and basic structure}

\begin{definition}[Divisor power sum]
For $N \ge 1$ and $k \ge 0$, $\sigma_k(N) = \sum_{d \mid N} d^{\,k}$. It is
multiplicative: $\sigma_k(MN) = \sigma_k(M)\sigma_k(N)$ for $\gcd(M,N)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Candidate witness]
For $k,a,b \ge 0$ set $W_k(a,b) = (1+a^k)(1+b^k)$, the value $\sigma_k$
\emph{would} take on $N = ab$ if $a$ and $b$ were distinct primes. Note
$W_k(a,b) = 1 + (a^k+b^k) + (ab)^k$ and $W_k(a,b) = W_k(b,a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Witness gap]
For a semiprime $N = pq$ set
\[
  G_k(p,q) = W_k(1,pq) - W_k(p,q) = (p^k-1)(q^k-1).
\]
Indeed $W_k(1,pq) = 2\bigl(1+(pq)^k\bigr)$ and $W_k(p,q) = 1+p^k+q^k+(pq)^k$,
whose difference is $1 - p^k - q^k + (pq)^k = (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Determination]
Fix $k,m$ and a semiprime $N = pq$. We say $\sigma_k \bmod m$ \emph{determines
the factorisation}, written $\Det(k,m;p,q)$, if for all non-negative integers
$a,b$ with $ab = pq$ and $W_k(a,b) \equiv W_k(p,q) \pmod m$ one has
$\{a,b\} = \{p,q\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Least determining modulus]
$m^\star_k(p,q) = \min\{ m \ge 1 : \Det(k,m;p,q)\}$, abbreviated $m^\star$ when
$k=2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Witness of a semiprime]
If $p \ne q$ are prime then $\sigma_k(pq) = (1+p^k)(1+q^k) = W_k(p,q)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$p$ and $q$ are coprime, so $\sigma_k(pq) = \sigma_k(p)\sigma_k(q)$ by
multiplicativity, and the divisors of a prime $p$ are $1$ and $p$, whence
$\sigma_k(p) = 1+p^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Factor pairs of a semiprime]\label{prop:pairs}
If $p,q$ are prime and $ab = pq$ with $a,b \ge 0$, then $(a,b)$ is one of
$(1,pq)$, $(p,q)$, $(q,p)$, $(pq,1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Neither $a$ nor $b$ is $0$. If $p \mid a$, write $a = pc$; cancelling $p$
gives $cb = q$, so by primality of $q$ either $c=1$, giving $(a,b) = (p,q)$,
or $c=q$ and $b=1$, giving $(a,b) = (pq,1)$. If $p \nmid a$ then $\gcd(p,a)=1$
and Euclid's lemma gives $a \mid q$, forcing $a=1$ (so $b=pq$) or $a=q$ (so
$b=p$).
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:pairs} is what makes the semiprime case rigid: there is
exactly one competing factorisation, the trivial one, hence exactly one gap.

\section{Rigidity of the full witness}

\begin{lemma}[Sum--product lemma]\label{lem:sp}
If $ab = cd$ and $a+b = c+d$ for non-negative integers, then $(a,b) = (c,d)$
or $(a,b) = (d,c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In $\mathbb{Z}$, $(a-c)(a-d) = a^2 - (c+d)a + cd = a^2 - (a+b)a + ab = 0$, so
$a = c$ or $a = d$; the sum condition then determines $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Witness rigidity, all orders]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $k \ge 1$ and let $a,b,c,d$ be non-negative integers with $ab = cd$ and
$W_k(a,b) = W_k(c,d)$. Then $\{a,b\} = \{c,d\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $ab=cd$ we get $a^kb^k = c^kd^k$. Expanding $W_k(a,b) = 1+a^k+b^k+(ab)^k$
and likewise for $(c,d)$, the constant and product terms cancel, leaving
$a^k+b^k = c^k+d^k$. Lemma~\ref{lem:sp} applied to $(a^k,b^k)$ and $(c^k,d^k)$
gives $\{a^k,b^k\} = \{c^k,d^k\}$, and $x \mapsto x^k$ is injective on
$\mathbb{N}$ for $k \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

Thus $(N,\sigma_k(N))$ is a complete invariant of the factor pair at every
order; the rest of the paper shows how redundantly it encodes it.

\section{Exact recovery at order two}

\begin{theorem}[Trace identity]\label{thm:trace}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N = pq$,
$\sigma_2(N) + 2N = (p+q)^2 + N^2 + 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sigma_2(N) = (1+p^2)(1+q^2) = 1+p^2+q^2+N^2$; add $2N = 2pq$ and use
$p^2+2pq+q^2 = (p+q)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Extracted trace]
$T(N,w) = \bigl\lfloor \sqrt{\,w + 2N - N^2 - 1\,}\bigr\rfloor$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N = pq$, $T(N,\sigma_2(N)) = p+q$ exactly, the
radicand being the perfect square $(p+q)^2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Recovery]\label{thm:recover}
Let $p<q$ be primes, $N = pq$, $t = T(N,\sigma_2(N))$ and $d =
\lfloor\sqrt{t^2-4N}\rfloor$. Then $p = (t-d)/2$ and $q = (t+d)/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$t = p+q$ by the previous corollary. The identity $(p+q)^2 = (q-p)^2 + 4pq$
shows $t^2-4N = (q-p)^2$, so $d = q-p$; then $(t-d)/2 = p$ and $(t+d)/2 = q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Recovered characteristic polynomial]
With $t = T(N,\sigma_2(N))$ as above, $X^2 - tX + N = (X-p)(X-q)$ in
$\mathbb{Z}[X]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $t = p+q$, $N = pq$ and expand.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$N = 187 = 11\cdot17$ has divisors $1,11,17,187$ and $\sigma_2(187) = 1 + 121
+ 289 + 34969 = 35380$. Then $35380 + 374 - 34969 - 1 = 784 = 28^2$, so
$t = 28$; $t^2-4N = 36 = 6^2$, so $d = 6$; and $(28 \mp 6)/2 = (11,17)$.
\end{example}

Recovery costs $O(M(n)\log n)$ bit operations on $n$-bit inputs, dominated by
two integer square roots. The entire difficulty lies in obtaining
$\sigma_2(N)$.

\section{The separation principle and the sharp threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Separation principle]\label{thm:sep}
Let $a,b,c,d \ge 0$ with $ab = cd$, and let $k,m \ge 0$. Then
\[
  W_k(a,b) \equiv W_k(c,d) \pmod m
  \iff m \mid \bigl((a^k+b^k) - (c^k+d^k)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$W_k(a,b) - W_k(c,d) = (a^k+b^k)-(c^k+d^k) + \bigl((ab)^k - (cd)^k\bigr)$ and
the final bracket vanishes.
\end{proof}

Interpretation: for a fixed $N$, the order-$k$ witness depends only on the
power-sum coordinate $a^k+b^k$. At $k=1$ this coordinate is the trace $a+b$.
The free witness therefore has a one-dimensional view of the factorisation,
and all threshold phenomena are shadows of that one-dimensionality.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp threshold theorem]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $p,q$ be primes, $N = pq$, and $k,m \ge 0$. Then
\[
  \Det(k,m;p,q) \iff m \nmid (p^k-1)(q^k-1) = G_k(p,q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $m \nmid G_k$ and let $ab = N$ with $W_k(a,b) \equiv
W_k(p,q) \pmod m$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:pairs}, $(a,b)$ is one of
$(1,N),(p,q),(q,p),(N,1)$. The middle two are the desired conclusion. In the
extreme cases, symmetry of $W_k$ turns the congruence into $W_k(1,N) \equiv
W_k(p,q) \pmod m$, i.e.\ $m \mid G_k$, a contradiction.

($\Rightarrow$) If $m \mid G_k$ then $W_k(1,N) \equiv W_k(p,q) \pmod m$, so
the trivial factorisation $(1,N)$ satisfies the congruence; as $p,q>1$ it is
not $\{p,q\}$, so determination fails.
\end{proof}

Two features deserve emphasis. First, there is \emph{no size condition}:
whether a modulus works is a purely arithmetic property of its interaction
with the single integer $G_k$. Second, the failure set $\{m : m \mid G_k\}$ is
closed under divisors, whence:

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:least}
$m^\star_k(p,q) = \min\{m \ge 1 : m \nmid (p^k-1)(q^k-1)\}$, the least
non-divisor of the witness gap. In particular $m^\star_k$ is at most the
smallest prime not dividing $G_k$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Refutation of the $\Theta(p+q)$ law}

\begin{proposition}
Let $p = 11$, $q = 17$, $N = 187$. Then $\Det(2,7;11,17)$ holds, although $7 <
p+q = 28$ and the conjectured threshold is $5(p+q) = 140$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$G_2 = 120 \cdot 288 = 34560$. Modulo $7$: $p^2 \equiv 2$ and $q^2 \equiv 2$,
so $G_2 \equiv 1 \cdot 1 = 1 \not\equiv 0$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\end{proof}

Concretely $\sigma_2(187) = 35380 \equiv 2 \pmod 7$, while the trivial
factorisation's witness $W_2(1,187) = 69940 \equiv 3 \pmod 7$.

\begin{theorem}[Constant-modulus theorem]\label{thm:const}
For every bound $B$ there exist primes $p,q$ with $B < p < q$ and
$\Det(2,7;p,q)$. Consequently $m^\star(p,q) \le 7$ for infinitely many
semiprimes with both prime factors arbitrarily large, and no lower bound of
the form $m^\star \ge c(p+q)$ with $c>0$ can hold.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Dirichlet's theorem the class $2 \bmod 7$ contains infinitely many primes.
Choose a prime $p > B$ with $p \equiv 2 \pmod 7$, then a prime $q > p$ with
$q \equiv 2 \pmod 7$. In $\mathbb{Z}/7$,
$G_2 = (p^2-1)(q^2-1) \equiv 3 \cdot 3 = 9 \equiv 2 \ne 0$, so $7 \nmid G_2$
and Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[What the experiment measured]
The empirical ratio $m^\star/(p+q) = 5.00$ is not a law of arithmetic. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:least}, $m^\star$ is the least non-divisor of $G_2$, which
is typically a very small number: a ``random'' integer fails to be divisible
by $5$ with probability $4/5$, so the least non-divisor is almost always below
$10$. A measurement proportional to $p+q$ must therefore have measured a
different quantity---for instance the least modulus that isolates the trace
among lifted candidates $t' = t + jm$ in a bounded window, a genuinely metric
question about a search procedure rather than about information content. Under
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} no enumeration of lifted traces is needed at all:
uniqueness is decided by a divisibility.
\end{remark}

\section{Locating the threshold exactly}

\subsection{A universal lower bound}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:24}
If $p$ is odd and $3 \nmid p$, then $p^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{24}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$p^2-1 = (p-1)(p+1)$ is a product of consecutive even integers, one divisible
by $4$, so $8 \mid p^2-1$; and $p \equiv \pm1 \pmod 3$ gives $3 \mid p^2-1$.
As $\gcd(8,3)=1$, $24 \mid p^2-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every prime $p>3$, $24 \mid p^2-1$; hence $24 \mid G_2(p,q)$ for any $q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Universal lower bound]\label{thm:lb}
Let $p,q$ be primes with $p>3$. Then no modulus $m$ with $1 \le m \le 4$
determines the factorisation of $pq$ from $\sigma_2 \bmod m$; that is,
$m^\star(p,q) \ge 5$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every $m \in \{1,2,3,4\}$ divides $24$, and $24 \mid G_2$, so $m \mid G_2$;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\end{proof}

Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:const}, the least determining modulus is pinned
between the absolute constants $5$ and (infinitely often) $7$. The constant
$5$ in the empirical law was real; the factor $(p+q)$ was not.

\subsection{The exact-five locus}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:five}
For $p \ge 1$, $5 \mid p^2-1 \iff p \equiv \pm 1 \pmod 5$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$p^2 \bmod 5$ depends only on $p \bmod 5$, and the squares are $0,1,4,4,1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact-five theorem]\label{thm:five}
For primes $p,q$,
\[
  \Det(2,5;p,q) \iff p \not\equiv \pm1 \ (\mathrm{mod}\ 5) \text{ and }
  q \not\equiv \pm1 \ (\mathrm{mod}\ 5).
\]
If moreover $p>3$, then under these conditions $m^\star(p,q) = 5$ exactly,
uniformly in the size of $p$ and $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, $\Det(2,5;p,q)$ iff $5 \nmid (p^2-1)(q^2-1)$;
since $5$ is prime this fails iff $5 \mid p^2-1$ or $5 \mid q^2-1$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:five} translates each. Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:lb}.
\end{proof}

Under independent equidistribution of $p,q$ over the invertible classes mod
$5$, the exact-five locus has density $1/4$ among semiprimes with $p,q>5$.

\subsection{Counting bounds}

\begin{lemma}
For $k \ge 1$ and primes $p,q$, $G_k(p,q) > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pigeonhole form]\label{thm:pigeon}
Let $k \ge 1$ and let $S$ be a finite set of distinct primes with $|S| >
\omega(G_k(p,q))$. Then some $r \in S$ satisfies $\Det(k,r;p,q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise every $r \in S$ divides $G_k$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, so $S$ is
contained in the set of prime divisors of $G_k$ and $|S| \le \omega(G_k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Product form]
Let $k \ge 1$ and let $S$ be a finite set of distinct primes with $G_k(p,q) <
2^{|S|}$. Then some $r \in S$ determines. In particular, if $G_k(p,q) <
2^{\pi(x)}$ then some prime $r<x$ determines.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise all $r \in S$ divide $G_k$, hence so does $\prod_{r \in S} r \ge
2^{|S|} > G_k > 0$, impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic bound on failures]\label{thm:log}
For $k \ge 1$, $\omega(G_k(p,q)) \le \log_2 G_k(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The product of the distinct prime divisors divides $G_k$ and is at least
$2^{\omega(G_k)}$, so $2^{\omega(G_k)} \le G_k$.
\end{proof}

For $k = 2$, $G_2 < N^2$, so at most $2\log_2 N$ prime moduli can fail and any
$2\log_2 N + 1$ distinct primes contain a working one.

\section{Order one: the totient and the RSA trapdoor}

\begin{proposition}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N = pq$, $G_1(p,q) = (p-1)(q-1) = \varphi(N)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Totient threshold]
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N = pq$,
$\Det(1,m;p,q) \iff m \nmid \varphi(N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} at $k=1$ combined with the previous proposition.
\end{proof}

This is a modular refinement of the classical equivalence ``knowing
$\varphi(N)$ is equivalent to factoring $N$'': the obstruction set for partial
information at order one is exactly the divisor lattice of $\varphi(N)$. Since
$\sigma_1(N) = N+1+t$ and $\varphi(N) = N+1-t$ with $t = p+q$, the two are two
faces of the same trace coordinate, exactly as the separation principle
predicts.

\begin{remark}[The totient tower]
At order $k$, $p^k - 1 = |\mathbb{F}_{p^k}^{\times}|$, so
$G_k(p,q) = |\mathbb{F}_{p^k}^{\times}| \cdot |\mathbb{F}_{q^k}^{\times}|$,
the product of the orders of the unit groups of the degree-$k$ extensions.
Since $p^k-1 \mid p^{kk'}-1$, the failure sets are nested along divisibility
of orders.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Trace recovery from the full witness]
\emph{Input:} $N$, $w = \sigma_2(N)$. \emph{Output:} $\{p,q\}$.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0pt]
\item $r \leftarrow w + 2N - N^2 - 1$.
\item $t \leftarrow \lfloor\sqrt r\rfloor$; abort unless $t^2 = r$.
\item $s \leftarrow t^2 - 4N$, $d \leftarrow \lfloor\sqrt s\rfloor$; abort
  unless $d^2 = s$.
\item Return $\bigl((t-d)/2,\ (t+d)/2\bigr)$.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:recover}; cost $O(M(n)\log n)$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Least determining modulus]
\emph{Input:} $p,q,k$. \emph{Output:} $m^\star_k(p,q)$.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0pt]
\item $G \leftarrow (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$.
\item For $m = 1,2,3,\dots$: if $G \bmod m \ne 0$, return $m$.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness is Corollary~\ref{cor:least}; the answer exceeds $m$ only if
$\lcm(1,\dots,m-1) \mid G$, so the loop exits after $O(1)$ iterations in
practice.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Separation test]
\emph{Input:} $N$, $k$, $m$, a residue $w_0 \equiv \sigma_k(N) \bmod m$, and
the divisor pairs of $N$. \emph{Output:} the factorisations consistent with
$w_0$. For each pair $(a,b)$ with $ab = N$, compute $W_k(a,b) \bmod m$ by
modular exponentiation and keep those congruent to $w_0$. This is a
verification procedure, not a factoring procedure.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Certified small-modulus search]
\emph{Input:} $p,q,k$, bound $x$. Compute $G = (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$ and return the
least prime $r<x$ with $r \nmid G$. By Theorems~\ref{thm:pigeon}
and~\ref{thm:log} the search succeeds whenever $\pi(x) > \log_2 G$; for $k=2$,
$\pi(x) > 2\log_2 N$ always suffices.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{The computational reading}

Every determination statement above is \emph{information-theoretic}: the
residue class $\sigma_k(N) \bmod m$ is compatible with exactly one
factorisation of $N$. It does not assert that the residue is computable
without knowing that factorisation.

The obstruction is the \emph{aggregation barrier}. The functional
$\sigma_k(N) = \sum_{d\mid N} d^k$ is a sum over the divisor set of $N$; all
known evaluations first obtain the prime factorisation and then apply
multiplicativity. Reduction modulo $m$ is a homomorphism applied \emph{after}
the sum has been formed; it shrinks the range of the summands but not the
index set of the aggregation.

The outcome is thus a refutation of the shortcut but a positive contribution
to barrier analysis. One might have hoped that partial values are cheap in
proportion to how partial they are, so that a $\Theta(p+q)$ informational
requirement would translate into a $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ computational task.
Theorem~\ref{thm:const} destroys the premise in the strongest direction: the
informational requirement collapses to a constant number of bits.
Consequently, any proof that divisor-sum aggregation is computationally
irreducible must show irreducibility for arbitrarily partial values---down to
$\sigma_2(N) \bmod 7$---and not merely for the full value.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The organising fact is the separation principle: two factorisations $N = ab =
cd$ are indistinguishable modulo $m$ exactly when $m \mid (a^k+b^k) -
(c^k+d^k)$. For semiprimes this collapses to the witness gap
$G_k(p,q) = (p^k-1)(q^k-1)$, and the threshold question becomes a divisibility
question, not a size question.

\begin{conjecture}[Logarithmic threshold, unconditional]
For every semiprime $N = pq$ with $p,q>3$, the least determining modulus
satisfies $m^\star(p,q) = O(\log N / \log\log N)$, and this order is attained
infinitely often.
\end{conjecture}

The failure set is $\{m : m \mid G_2\}$, so $m^\star$ is the least non-divisor
of a number below $N^2$; it is bounded by the first prime not dividing $G_2$,
and $G_2$ has at most $\log_2 G_2$ distinct prime divisors. The counting half
is Theorems~\ref{thm:pigeon} and~\ref{thm:log}; what remains is an effective
Chebyshev-type lower bound for $\pi(x)$ to convert this into an explicit
$x = O(\log N \log\log N)$, and then a sharpening.

\begin{conjecture}[Exact density of the $m^\star = 5$ locus]
Under independent equidistribution of $p,q$ over the invertible residues mod
$5$, the set of semiprimes with $m^\star = 5$ has natural density $1/4$; more
generally $\Pr[m^\star = r]$ for a prime $r$ is a product over smaller primes
of the corresponding local failure probabilities.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:five} converts $m^\star = 5$ into the purely local condition
$p,q \not\equiv \pm1 \pmod 5$, so the density question becomes a Dirichlet
equidistribution question one prime at a time; empirical histograms with mass
concentrated on $5$, $7$ and $11$ should match the product formula.

\begin{conjecture}[Order-$k$ collapse to the totient tower]
For each $k \ge 1$, $G_k(p,q) =
|\mathbb{F}_{p^k}^{\times}|\cdot|\mathbb{F}_{q^k}^{\times}|$, so the modular
threshold at order $k$ is governed by the orders of the unit groups of the
degree-$k$ extensions; the failure sets are nested along the divisibility
lattice of orders, whence $m^\star_k \le m^\star_{kk'}$. Along highly divisible chains such as
$k = \lcm(1,\dots,n)$ one expects $m^\star_k \to \infty$, namely
$m^\star_k$ is the least prime $r$ with $\operatorname{ord}_r(p) \nmid k$ and
$\operatorname{ord}_r(q) \nmid k$; note that $m^\star_k$ is not monotone in
$k$ itself.
\end{conjecture}

Two further directions: the analogue for $N$ with more than two prime factors,
where the least determining modulus becomes the least non-divisor of a
\emph{set} of gaps---a covering-system question; and the multi-modulus
version, where determination from residues modulo $m_1,\dots,m_r$ holds iff
$\lcm(m_i) \nmid G_k$, making the lcm lattice the natural object.

\section{Conclusion}

The free witness of a semiprime carries its factorisation completely and
redundantly. Completeness is the rigidity theorem: the pair (product, witness)
is a complete invariant at every order. Redundancy is the sharp threshold
theorem: the residue modulo $m$ suffices whenever $m$ fails to divide
$(p^k-1)(q^k-1)$, a condition of pure divisibility with no size constraint.
Consequently the conjectured trace-order threshold $m^\star \approx 5(p+q)$ is
false; the fixed modulus $7$ suffices for infinitely many semiprimes with
arbitrarily large factors, while $m^\star \ge 5$ always, with equality exactly
when neither factor is $\pm1$ modulo $5$. At order one the obstruction is
Euler's totient, making the RSA trapdoor visible as the divisor lattice of
$\varphi(N)$. Since a constant number of bits suffices informationally, the
entire hardness of this route resides in the aggregation step.

\end{document}

